<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aba Women's War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1929</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Women's War of 1929, Aba riots</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aba Women's War was a revolt by thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> and Ibibio women in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> in November and December 1929. Sparked by fears that women would be taxed, it targeted the warrant chiefs appointed under British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>; women gathered to 'sit on' chiefs and attacked courts and trading stores. Colonial troops killed more than 50 women, and the protest led to reforms of local administration.</p></div>
